    {
      "heading": "Double negatives are normal",
      "body": "If a negative word comes **after** the verb, you need *no* before the verb: *No hay nadie.* (There's nobody.) *No quiero nada.* (I don't want anything.) *No voy nunca.* If the negative word comes **first**, drop *no*: *Nadie vino.* (Nobody came.) *Nunca voy.*\n\nSo in questions with \"any\": *¿Quieres algo?* — *No, no quiero nada.*"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Alguno and ninguno",
      "body": "They agree with the noun and shorten before a masculine singular noun: *algún libro, ningún problema*. *Ninguno* is almost always singular: *No tengo ningún amigo aquí* (I don't have any friends here). *¿Tienes alguna pregunta?* — *No, ninguna.*\n\n*Alguien / nadie* refer to people and take personal *a* as objects: *¿Conoces a alguien aquí? — No, no conozco a nadie.*\n\nA very common fixed phrase: *No tengo ni idea* (I have no idea)."
    }
  ],
